{"id": "2026-11-27-news-eu-budget-summit-breaks-up-without-deal", "date": "2026-11-27", "stream": "news", "headline": "EU budget summit breaks up without deal", "summary": "EU leaders ended the special European Council on the 2028-2034 budget a day early on 26 November without agreement; the next attempt is the 17 December summit.", "url": "https://www.tagesschau.de/ausland/europa/eu-haushalt-vorzeitig-beendet-100.html", "source_domain": "tagesschau.de", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["politics", "economy"], "importance": 3, "display_body": "EU leaders broke off their special budget summit in Brussels late on Thursday 26 November, a day early and without agreement on the 2028–2034 budget (the multiannual financial framework, or MFF). The next attempt is the regular summit on 17 December. German Chancellor Friedrich Merz announced the early end and said the second day, planned for Friday, would not take place. The latest proposal is about €1.7 trillion over seven years, down from roughly €2 trillion at the start and more than €200 billion below that. Merz wants a few hundred billion more in cuts, rules out new joint EU borrowing and still ties his consent to Ukraine aid. He called the €200 billion cut a \"lower limit\". Germany now negotiates alongside the Netherlands, Austria, Sweden, Finland, Denmark and Belgium, a group that calls itself the \"reasonable seven\". On the other side, net recipients in the south and east, and net payers France and Italy, want to protect farm and regional spending. Italian Prime Minister Giorgia Meloni said the sides were \"light years\" apart. French President Emmanuel Macron said further cuts would be a \"strategic mistake\" that leaves EU investment falling behind China and the US, and promised to defend the full farm-policy budget. RFI reports that the frugal group would not even join one of the small-format sessions run by Council President António Costa.", "why": "the MFF sets what the EU can spend on farms, regions, defence and Ukraine for seven years, and the EU programmes Switzerland pays to join, such as Horizon Europe research funding, run on it. A deal slipping past 17 December would push the fight into the run-up to France's 2027 presidential election.", "thread_id": "2026-11-27-news-eu-budget-summit-breaks-up-without-deal", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-27", "event_date": "2026-11-26", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["European Union", "European Council", "Friedrich Merz", "Giorgia Meloni", "Emmanuel Macron"]}
{"id": "2026-11-27-news-hern-ndez-de-cos-tapped-to-lead-the-ecb", "date": "2026-11-27", "stream": "news", "headline": "Hernández de Cos tapped to lead the ECB", "summary": "The European Council put forward Pablo Hernández de Cos as next ECB president to succeed Christine Lagarde, plus Laurence Boone and Markus Brunnermeier for the executive board.", "url": "https://www.consilium.europa.eu/en/press/press-releases/2026/11/26/european-council-puts-forward-pablo-hernandez-de-cos-as-next-president-of-the-european-central-bank/", "source_domain": "consilium.europa.eu", "tier": "T1", "tags": ["official PR", "via snippet"], "topics": ["economy"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "On Thursday 26 November EU leaders put forward Pablo Hernández de Cos, former governor of the Banco de España, as the next president of the European Central Bank (ECB). They also named Laurence Boone and Markus Brunnermeier for the ECB's executive board. He would succeed Christine Lagarde, whose term ends in February, and would be the first Spaniard in the job. Tagesschau reports that analysts see the 55-year-old economist as a moderate \"dove\", meaning he leans towards looser monetary policy. He recently said, however, that the hope of a quick end to the Iran war and lower energy prices had not come true, so the ECB must \"remain vigilant\" on inflation, which is at its highest in more than three years.", "why": "ECB policy moves the euro–franc exchange rate and so the Swiss National Bank's room for manoeuvre. A dove taking over during an energy-driven inflation spike is the scenario markets will watch.", "thread_id": "2026-11-27-news-hern-ndez-de-cos-tapped-to-lead-the-ecb", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-27", "event_date": "2026-11-26", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["European Central Bank", "Pablo Hernández de Cos", "Christine Lagarde"]}
{"id": "2026-11-27-news-france-slips-into-recession", "date": "2026-11-27", "stream": "news", "headline": "France slips into recession", "summary": "Insee revised French Q3 2026 GDP to -0.2% after a slightly negative Q2, putting France in technical recession, with the interim deficit at 5.6% of GDP.", "url": "https://www.insee.fr/fr/statistiques/9165918", "source_domain": "insee.fr", "tier": "T1", "tags": ["official PR"], "topics": ["economy"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "France is in recession: revised figures from the national statistics office Insee on Friday 27 November show GDP fell 0.2% in the third quarter, worse than the −0.1% first published, after a second quarter that Insee now says was slightly negative. Insee's table still rounds the second quarter to 0.0%. Le Monde quotes Marie Leclair, head of Insee's national accounts department, as saying second-quarter output fell \"very slightly\", enough to say \"we have a recession\" (two consecutive quarters of contraction). Exports fell 1.6% and business investment fell again, by 0.9%, while household consumption rose 0.3%. Growth already locked in for 2026 is just 0.1%, against the government's 0.5% hope. Le Monde puts the interim public deficit at 5.6% of GDP, above the 5.4% target, though Insee notes that fourth-quarter tax receipts usually narrow it.", "why": "France is a top-five Swiss trading partner and the economy next door to Vaud and Geneva, where many cross-border workers live. A recession with a widening deficit also hardens Paris's line in the EU budget fight above.", "thread_id": "2026-11-27-news-france-slips-into-recession", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-27", "event_date": "2026-11-27", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["France", "Insee"]}
{"id": "2026-11-27-news-drone-kills-two-italian-technicians-in-kuwait", "date": "2026-11-27", "stream": "news", "headline": "Drone kills two Italian technicians in Kuwait", "summary": "A drone attack on a Kuwaiti military site on 26 November killed two Rheinmetall Italia technicians servicing a Skyguard air-defence system; Italy blamed Iran, which denied it.", "url": "https://www.esteri.it/it/sala_stampa/archivionotizie/comunicati/2026/11/kuwait-il-ministro-degli-esteri-sheikh-jarrah-al-sabah-telefona-a-tajani-solidarieta-allitalia-per-la-morte-dei-due-connazionali/", "source_domain": "esteri.it", "tier": "T1", "tags": ["official PR", "new source", "disputed"], "topics": ["geopolitics"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "A drone attack on a Kuwaiti military site on Thursday 26 November killed two Italian technicians working for Rheinmetall Italia, the Italian branch of the German arms maker. Italy blamed Iran; Iran's armed forces denied it. Italy's foreign ministry says the men were maintaining antennas of the Skyguard air-defence system when the blast hit. Skyguard is the anti-aircraft fire-control system originally developed by the Swiss firm Oerlikon-Contraves, now part of Rheinmetall. Kuwait's defence ministry said a Kuwaiti and a Pakistani national were also injured. Foreign Minister Antonio Tajani told the Italian Senate that several drones of Iranian origin had targeted Kuwaiti military facilities. An Iranian army spokesman said it may have been a \"false flag\" operation, and Iran's foreign ministry offered to take part in an investigation. Kuwait's foreign minister phoned Tajani to express solidarity. Kuwait, a US ally, has been hit repeatedly since the US and Israel began the war on Iran at the end of February.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-11-27-news-drone-kills-two-italian-technicians-in-kuwait", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-27", "event_date": "2026-11-26", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Kuwait", "Italy", "Iran", "Rheinmetall", "Skyguard"]}
{"id": "2026-11-27-news-new-zealand-coalition-one-seat-short", "date": "2026-11-27", "stream": "news", "headline": "New Zealand coalition one seat short", "summary": "Final results of New Zealand's 7 November election left Luxon's National-led coalition with 65 of 130 seats, one short of a majority, making the centrist Opportunity party kingmaker.", "url": "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/nov/27/new-zealand-nz-election-2026-final-results", "source_domain": "theguardian.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["politics"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "The final count in New Zealand's 7 November election, released on Friday 27 November, left Prime Minister Christopher Luxon's coalition with 65 of 130 seats, one short of a majority. The coalition is made up of Luxon's National party, the populist NZ First and the libertarian ACT. The Electoral Commission's count of nearly 485,000 \"special votes\", cast from overseas or outside a voter's electorate, cost National two seats, according to the Guardian. That leaves National 40, Labour 36, Greens 17, ACT 13, NZ First 12, the new centrist Opportunity party 7 and Te Pāti Māori 5. Three electorate seats changed hands, one of them by just 81 votes. National can request recounts until 3 December, and winning back one seat would restore its majority. Luxon, who had earlier ruled out working with Opportunity, said National will open formal talks with NZ First, ACT and Opportunity, and warned they would be \"challenging\". If no majority emerges, New Zealand faces a fresh election, though Reuters, via the Japan Times, reports that analysts consider that unlikely.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-11-27-news-new-zealand-coalition-one-seat-short", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-27", "event_date": "2026-11-27", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["New Zealand", "Christopher Luxon", "Opportunity Party"]}
{"id": "2026-11-27-news-eisenkot-signs-pact-with-israel-s-democrats", "date": "2026-11-27", "stream": "news", "headline": "Eisenkot signs pact with Israel's Democrats", "summary": "Gadi Eisenkot's Yashar party signed a coalition agreement with Yair Golan's Democrats on 26 November, giving the nascent coalition 36 of the 61 seats needed before a 6 December deadline.", "url": "https://www.rfi.fr/fr/moyen-orient/20261126-isra%C3%ABl-gadi-eisenkot-et-le-parti-de-gauche-nouent-un-accord-de-coalition", "source_domain": "rfi.fr", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["single-source"], "topics": ["politics", "geopolitics"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "Gadi Eisenkot's Yashar party, charged with forming Israel's next government, signed a coalition agreement with Yair Golan's Democrats on Thursday 26 November. The agreement gives the Democrats four ministries, including national security, now held by Itamar Ben Gvir. With Avigdor Liberman's party, which signed on Tuesday 24 November, the nascent coalition has 36 seats, far short of the 61 needed in the Knesset. The government must be formed by 6 December. The Democrats are pushing for a minority government backed by two majority-Arab lists. They oppose the four rounds of exploratory talks Yashar has held with Benjamin Netanyahu's Likud on a unity government.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-11-27-news-eisenkot-signs-pact-with-israel-s-democrats", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-27", "event_date": "2026-11-26", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Israel", "Gadi Eisenkot", "Yair Golan", "Benjamin Netanyahu"]}
{"id": "2026-11-27-news-guinea-bissau-junta-delays-vote-to-2028", "date": "2026-11-27", "stream": "news", "headline": "Guinea-Bissau junta delays vote to 2028", "summary": "Guinea-Bissau's military junta postponed the presidential election by two years to 26 November 2028, on the first anniversary of its coup.", "url": "https://www.dw.com/en/guinea-bissau-junta-delays-presidential-vote-for-2-years/a-80068910", "source_domain": "dw.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["single-source"], "topics": ["politics"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "Guinea-Bissau's military junta postponed the presidential election by two years, to 26 November 2028, on Thursday 26 November, exactly one year after it seized power. The vote had been due on 6 December. Transitional president General Horta Inta-a cited \"an insufficiency of the technical, material and financial conditions\". The military took over three days after the November 2025 general election and arrested President Umaro Sissoco Embaló before results were released, while he and opposition candidate Fernando Dias da Costa both claimed victory. The West African bloc ECOWAS has urged a return to civilian rule.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-11-27-news-guinea-bissau-junta-delays-vote-to-2028", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-27", "event_date": "2026-11-26", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Guinea-Bissau", "Horta Inta-a", "ECOWAS"]}
{"id": "2026-11-27-news-bern-admits-failings-over-kabuga-visa", "date": "2026-11-27", "stream": "news", "headline": "Bern admits failings over Kabuga visa", "summary": "A Federal Council report on postulate 23.4287 found the federal aliens office head acted arbitrarily in granting Rwandan genocide suspect Félicien Kabuga a visa in 1994, though his expulsion was lawful.", "url": "https://www.admin.ch/fr/newnsb/vnJhm6kXC02Q", "source_domain": "admin.ch", "tier": "T1", "tags": ["official PR"], "topics": ["switzerland"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "The Federal Council admitted on Thursday 26 November that Swiss authorities did not act properly in 1994 when Félicien Kabuga, an alleged financier of the Rwandan genocide, entered Switzerland. It found the then head of the Federal Aliens Office used his discretion \"arbitrarily\" in granting the visa. The visa for Kabuga and eight relatives was issued on 9 June 1994, while the genocide was under way, and the family entered legally on 22 July, despite repeated attempts by the foreign ministry to have the visa cancelled. The report answers National Council postulate 23.4287 and draws on a University of Basel historical study. It finds that expelling Kabuga in August 1994 was lawful, because officials judged the evidence too thin for a prosecution in Switzerland to succeed. It also says the decision weighed domestic fears of a suspected war criminal living freely in the country more heavily than Switzerland's international responsibility. The government says another decision \"would have been possible\" and that foreign-policy considerations must carry more weight in future cases with international-law implications. Kabuga evaded arrest until 2020 in France.", "why": "it is a rare formal government admission that Swiss migration authorities failed in a genocide case, and it sets a stated rule for weighing foreign-policy duties in future visa and expulsion decisions.", "thread_id": "2026-11-27-news-bern-admits-failings-over-kabuga-visa", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-27", "event_date": "2026-11-26", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Félicien Kabuga", "Federal Council", "Rwanda"]}
{"id": "2026-11-27-news-rents-climb-to-second-in-swiss-worry-list", "date": "2026-11-27", "stream": "news", "headline": "Rents climb to second in Swiss worry list", "summary": "The UBS Worry Barometer 2026 by gfs.bern found 48% of Swiss voters name health costs and premiums as their top worry, with housing costs rising to second at 30%.", "url": "https://www.srf.ch/news/schweiz/ubs-sorgenbarometer-2026-krankenkassenpraemien-bleiben-die-groesste-sorge-der-schweizer", "source_domain": "srf.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["single-source"], "topics": ["switzerland"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Health costs and insurance premiums remain Swiss voters' top worry for 2026, named by 48%, and housing costs and rents jumped to second place at 30%, according to the UBS Worry Barometer published on Friday 27 November. The survey of 2,001 voters, conducted in June and July by the research institute gfs.bern for UBS, puts environment and climate third (28%), followed by pensions (24%) and immigration (23%), all slightly down on 2025. Digitalisation and AI made the biggest jump, from 30th to 16th place. Only 1% named financial-system stability. In the 50th-anniversary edition, 51% said they would rather live in the Switzerland of 1976 than that of 2076 (24%).", "why": "rent overtaking pensions and immigration as a concern sets the agenda for the next housing and tenancy-law fights in parliament.", "thread_id": "2026-11-27-news-rents-climb-to-second-in-swiss-worry-list", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-27", "event_date": "2026-11-27", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["UBS", "gfs.bern"]}
{"id": "2026-11-27-news-cantons-enforced-87-of-expulsions-in-2025", "date": "2026-11-27", "stream": "news", "headline": "Cantons enforced 87% of expulsions in 2025", "summary": "SEM said cantons carried out 1,748 of 2,021 enforceable criminal expulsions in 2025, about 87%.", "url": "https://www.admin.ch/fr/newnsb/ZMWuz7xWSwIz", "source_domain": "admin.ch", "tier": "T1", "tags": ["official PR"], "topics": ["switzerland"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "The cantons carried out 1,748 of the 2,021 criminal expulsions that became enforceable in 2025, about 87%, the State Secretariat for Migration (SEM) said on Friday 27 November. Expulsion is a court-imposed penalty for foreign offenders that bars re-entry to Switzerland and the Schengen area for a set period. Of those expelled, 74% were removed under compulsion and 26% left on their own; 38% were EU/EFTA nationals. Algeria (15%), Romania (11%) and Albania (10%) were the most common countries of origin. SEM says the rate will rise further because some late-2025 cases were only carried out in 2026. It also issued 8,967 entry bans in 2025.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-11-27-news-cantons-enforced-87-of-expulsions-in-2025", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-27", "event_date": "2026-11-27", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["SEM"]}
{"id": "2026-11-27-news-swiss-and-french-ministers-to-sign-space-pact", "date": "2026-11-27", "stream": "news", "headline": "Swiss and French ministers to sign space pact", "summary": "Federal Councillor Martin Pfister and French minister Catherine Vautrin were due to sign a letter of intent on military space cooperation in Bern on 27 November.", "url": "https://www.admin.ch/fr/newnsb/pu4R3zCBrshR", "source_domain": "admin.ch", "tier": "T1", "tags": ["official PR"], "topics": ["switzerland", "security"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "Defence minister Martin Pfister was due to sign a letter of intent on military space cooperation with French Armed Forces Minister Catherine Vautrin in Bern on Friday 27 November, the defence department (DDPS) announced. It covers information exchange, training and research, and the DDPS says it will respect Swiss neutrality. The ministers were also to discuss European security architecture, hybrid threats and air-defence procurement.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-11-27-news-swiss-and-french-ministers-to-sign-space-pact", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-27", "event_date": "2026-11-27", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Martin Pfister", "Catherine Vautrin", "DDPS", "France"]}
